  "brief_description": "We are looking through an arched window of a brick house at a well-dressed young man. He holds a heart-shaped book; a pen case and inkwell sit on the window ledge. This tiny painting was possibly once part of a diptych (a painting made of two parts). The young man may be at his devotions, and might originally have been gazing at a religious image in the missing left wing.\n\nThere are no obvious indications who the sitter was. He may have been a scribe, though clearly -- considering his clothes and jewellery -- an affluent one. Behind him is the church of Our Lady of the Zavel and a view towards the walls of Brussels. This is one of the earliest known views of the city and an early example of a relatively exact rendering of a recognisable place.",
